(2679475, 365935, 15358, '<td valign="top">I''ll go along with you that the 1''s, 2''s and 3''s are tough scores.  The biggest problem that I see is the lighting.  It looks like you had the flashes on both sides of him,  it makes the shadows look strange.<br/> <br/></td>', 'I''ll go along with you that the 1''s, 2''s and 3''s are tough scores.  The biggest problem that I see is the lighting.  It looks like you had the flashes on both sides of him,  it makes the shadows look strange. ', '2006-07-31 11:04:36.000000', NULL, 0),
(2679672, 364633, 5354, '<td valign="top">Thanks for all the comment everyone, glad you liked the image.<br/>Just to give you a little background I planned to go away that weekend with the family, but as soon as the challenge was set I knew we had to go to the beach.<br/> <br/>I checked Tide Tables and Sunrise times to give me a narrower choice of locations. It came down to Northumberland for dawn shots or Cumbria for Sunset. Northumberland it was.<br/> <br/>So we packed up the kids and set off on the Friday night, drove 3hrs north and checked into teh hotel. At 3:00am I was up again and heading down to the beach ready for dawn. Only to be met by 15 other photographers all pointing cameras at Bamburgh Castle (my first choice).<br/> <br/>Quickly looking round I opted for the lighthouse, which everyone else was ignoring.<br/> <br/>I set the tripod up on an exposed rock and waited for dawn. The incoming tide got closer and closer, then started to lap around the bottom of the tripod. Still no sunrise. By the time I took the shot the water was 9 inches up the tripod legs and almost upto my knees. The North Sea is COLD ;-)<br/> <br/>Used several cokin P ND filters and 2 ND grads to get the long exposure for the sea, whilst maintaing the correct sky exposure. Oh then I dropped the cable release into the Sea too.<br/> <br/>All in all an expensive image, but worth every penny.<br/> <br/>BTW - if youe want to see the final result of a similar image fully worked up in PS then go <a href="//www.pbase.com/falcn/image/63643679" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">here</a><br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2006-07-31 11:03:59.</i></td>', 'Thanks for all the comment everyone, glad you liked the image.Just to give you a little background I planned to go away that weekend with the family, but as soon as the challenge was set I knew we had to go to the beach. I checked Tide Tables and Sunrise times to give me a narrower choice of locations. It came down to Northumberland for dawn shots or Cumbria for Sunset. Northumberland it was. So we packed up the kids and set off on the Friday night, drove 3hrs north and checked into teh hotel. At 3:00am I was up again and heading down to the beach ready for dawn. Only to be met by 15 other photographers all pointing cameras at Bamburgh Castle (my first choice). Quickly looking round I opted for the lighthouse, which everyone else was ignoring. I set the tripod up on an exposed rock and waited for dawn. The incoming tide got closer and closer, then started to lap around the bottom of the tripod. Still no sunrise. By the time I took the shot the water was 9 inches up the tripod legs and almost upto my knees. The North Sea is COLD ;-) Used several cokin P ND filters and 2 ND grads to get the long exposure for the sea, whilst maintaing the correct sky exposure. Oh then I dropped the cable release into the Sea too. All in all an expensive image, but worth every penny. BTW - if youe want to see the final result of a similar image fully worked up in PS then go here', '2006-07-31 12:02:57.000000', '2006-07-31 12:03:59.000000', 0),
(2679755, 365804, 5354, '<td valign="top">Well worth the 8 I gave it. Would have been higher if the surf hadn''t burnt out.</td>', 'Well worth the 8 I gave it. Would have been higher if the surf hadn''t burnt out.', '2006-07-31 12:28:35.000000', NULL, 0),
(2679882, 365394, 21816, '<td valign="top"><b>* Greetings from the Critique Club *</b> <br/> <br/>Hi Amy. It was fortunate that you were on vacation at the beach when this challenge came up and you chose a great subject for it.<br/> <br/>Two things come to mind as to how this photo could of been improved upon and both have been mentioned in previous comments. Framing is important and placing the lovely shell smack in the middle of the shot probably hurt your score quite a bit. I know it''s filling the frame but maybe if you had of pulled it back a tad and used the rule of thirds to compose it to add a bit of impact to it.<br/> <br/>I understand that by your comment you didn''t really have access to proper editing software. This is a real shame as the top half of the photo really needed some cleaning up. There''s some serious noise and artifacts around the shell and the ocean which no doubt would of affected votes too.<br/> <br/>I really love the colours you''ve captured and they really stand out well. Considering it looks like you''ve shot in full sunlight I wouldn''t be too concerned about the few blown highlights on the front of the shell. You''ve kept them pretty well under control. You''ve also got some wonderfully crisp focus on the shell too.<br/> <br/>All up, well done on your 2nd entry. It scored well and placed well. You should be proud. Best of luck in future challenges.<br/> <br/>Neil</td>', '* Greetings from the Critique Club *  Hi Amy. It was fortunate that you were on vacation at the beach when this challenge came up and you chose a great subject for it. Two things come to mind as to how this photo could of been improved upon and both have been mentioned in previous comments. Framing is important and placing the lovely shell smack in the middle of the shot probably hurt your score quite a bit. I know it''s filling the frame but maybe if you had of pulled it back a tad and used the rule of thirds to compose it to add a bit of impact to it. I understand that by your comment you didn''t really have access to proper editing software. This is a real shame as the top half of the photo really needed some cleaning up. There''s some serious noise and artifacts around the shell and the ocean which no doubt would of affected votes too. I really love the colours you''ve captured and they really stand out well. Considering it looks like you''ve shot in full sunlight I wouldn''t be too concerned about the few blown highlights on the front of the shell. You''ve kept them pretty well under control. You''ve also got some wonderfully crisp focus on the shell too. All up, well done on your 2nd entry. It scored well and placed well. You should be proud. Best of luck in future challenges. Neil', '2006-07-31 13:07:41.000000', NULL, 0),
(2680599, 366155, 60779, '<td valign="top">Thanks for the helpful comment Joe. I''ll take an in-depth look at the tutorial. re: size, I was trying different combinations of size vs. image quality, saving at 10, 11, 12. <br/> <br/>Josh<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2006-07-31 15:14:08.</i></td>', 'Thanks for the helpful comment Joe. I''ll take an in-depth look at the tutorial. re: size, I was trying different combinations of size vs. image quality, saving at 10, 11, 12.  Josh', '2006-07-31 16:01:53.000000', '2006-07-31 16:14:08.000000', 0),
(2681114, 366015, 50641, '<td valign="top">This is more zen than any of the zen challenge feathers.</td>', 'This is more zen than any of the zen challenge feathers.', '2006-07-31 18:32:36.000000', NULL, 0),
(2681488, 365198, 25218, '<td valign="top"><b>&lt;&lt; Critique Club &gt;&gt;</b> <br/> <br/><b>Initial Impression:</b> <br/>"Is that some kind of bone? Oh! It''s the foam in stop-action! Pretty cool!" Honestly, I thought it was a bone. <br/> <br/><b>Composition:</b> <br/>Compositionally, it seems just a little off or unbalanced - a bit heavy on the right side, maybe. I do like that the c-shape of the foam is echoed by the surf still on the beach. But then again, the wow factor of this photo doesn''t come from the composition, does it? <br/> <br/><b>Color/Tone:</b> <br/>Beautiful beach coloring - cool blues, golden sand &amp; white foam. <br/> <br/><b>Focus/Exposure:</b> <br/>As a commenter said, I like how the depth of field keeps the eye up in the foreground where the action is. To nit-pick, to my eyes, the focus seems to be on the sand dune at the top right of the photo, instead of on the foam, but it''s not a terrible thing and it''s not all that noticeable.<br/> <br/><b>Title:</b> <br/>It''s a bit blah, but it helps the viewer realize what exactly it is they''re looking at (well, just me, maybe)..<br/> <br/><b>Meets the Challenge:</b> <br/>Duh.<br/> <br/><b>Summary:</b> <br/>Although the slightly strained composition and slightly soft focus gives me the impression that this wasn''t exactly what you planned to shoot, it is definitely an amazing image with a well-deserved score!</td>', '<< Critique Club >>  Initial Impression: "Is that some kind of bone? Oh! It''s the foam in stop-action! Pretty cool!" Honestly, I thought it was a bone.  Composition: Compositionally, it seems just a little off or unbalanced - a bit heavy on the right side, maybe. I do like that the c-shape of the foam is echoed by the surf still on the beach. But then again, the wow factor of this photo doesn''t come from the composition, does it?  Color/Tone: Beautiful beach coloring - cool blues, golden sand & white foam.  Focus/Exposure: As a commenter said, I like how the depth of field keeps the eye up in the foreground where the action is. To nit-pick, to my eyes, the focus seems to be on the sand dune at the top right of the photo, instead of on the foam, but it''s not a terrible thing and it''s not all that noticeable. Title: It''s a bit blah, but it helps the viewer realize what exactly it is they''re looking at (well, just me, maybe).. Meets the Challenge: Duh. Summary: Although the slightly strained composition and slightly soft focus gives me the impression that this wasn''t exactly what you planned to shoot, it is definitely an amazing image with a well-deserved score!', '2006-07-31 20:22:11.000000', NULL, 0),
(2681520, 365427, 12448, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Why yes, I can see the Barracuda! ;)  Very nice.<br/> <br/>This is a good shot and with a little work could be a fantastic shot.  I like the composition and the idea behind the shot.  Nice eye catching the image in the driftwood.<br/> <br/>But two things caught my eye almost immediately, first the feel of the shot, working in the direct sun can be a nightmare and this is one of those instances.  Looking up your camera I see you can adjust the aperture settings and when working with bright light like this you might want to close your aperture just another notch or two.  Make it a little darker and work with it later in Photoshop if you need.  Or even try a couple of different settings to see how they come out.  The hardest thing to learn with a camera is lighting.  I''m still fighting with mine most of the time.<br/> <br/>Next was the horizon, even though you did a great job with your DOF, you kept my attention on the wood and still had enough detail to the ocean to make it a viable part of the picture, the horizon is very crooked.  A problem in that it throws the whole shot off but how would the driftwood look if you tilted it to correct the ocean? Well, using my magic envelopes, I took off the bottom of the picture and the peice of wood right behind the Barracuda so all I could see was the ocean and the part of the driftwood that is the Barracuda.  What a difference this made in the shot. I didn''t have all this other stuff fighting for my attention and the wood really stands out against the ocean, making one think the barracuda was jumping from the ocean.  Maybe if you were a little lower and reframed your shot?  And watch your horizon.<br/> <br/>One last thing, the title.  BARRACUDA!!  would have been plenty.  If you can use less words, do ;)<br/> <br/>Well, looking at your profile, this is your 4th challenge, let me say Welcome to DPChallenge and keep it up, I really like your ship shot.<br/> <br/>I hope my comments help and Good Luck in future Challenges!<br/> <br/>Deannda</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! Why yes, I can see the Barracuda! ;)  Very nice. This is a good shot and with a little work could be a fantastic shot.  I like the composition and the idea behind the shot.  Nice eye catching the image in the driftwood. But two things caught my eye almost immediately, first the feel of the shot, working in the direct sun can be a nightmare and this is one of those instances.  Looking up your camera I see you can adjust the aperture settings and when working with bright light like this you might want to close your aperture just another notch or two.  Make it a little darker and work with it later in Photoshop if you need.  Or even try a couple of different settings to see how they come out.  The hardest thing to learn with a camera is lighting.  I''m still fighting with mine most of the time. Next was the horizon, even though you did a great job with your DOF, you kept my attention on the wood and still had enough detail to the ocean to make it a viable part of the picture, the horizon is very crooked.  A problem in that it throws the whole shot off but how would the driftwood look if you tilted it to correct the ocean? Well, using my magic envelopes, I took off the bottom of the picture and the peice of wood right behind the Barracuda so all I could see was the ocean and the part of the driftwood that is the Barracuda.  What a difference this made in the shot. I didn''t have all this other stuff fighting for my attention and the wood really stands out against the ocean, making one think the barracuda was jumping from the ocean.  Maybe if you were a little lower and reframed your shot?  And watch your horizon. One last thing, the title.  BARRACUDA!!  would have been plenty.  If you can use less words, do ;) Well, looking at your profile, this is your 4th challenge, let me say Welcome to DPChallenge and keep it up, I really like your ship shot. I hope my comments help and Good Luck in future Challenges! Deannda', '2006-07-31 20:29:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(2681677, 364293, 22301, '<td valign="top">kelli, <br/>Thanks for your comment on my on the beach entry and I am now coming back to give you a comment:).<br/>I think this entry is very well composed as my eye is drawn to the boy from the rule of thirds as well as from the line of the wave to which runs to the head of the boy. The only thing I could suggest would myabe to have used a wider angle or lowere perpective to get more of the beach area and possibly the horizon. A more open composition may have helped show the concentration and focus of the boy also. It may also have lost impact with the boy smaller in the phot also I am not sure. Just a suggestion. Thanks again for your comment. <br/>Joe</td>', 'kelli, Thanks for your comment on my on the beach entry and I am now coming back to give you a comment:).I think this entry is very well composed as my eye is drawn to the boy from the rule of thirds as well as from the line of the wave to which runs to the head of the boy. The only thing I could suggest would myabe to have used a wider angle or lowere perpective to get more of the beach area and possibly the horizon. A more open composition may have helped show the concentration and focus of the boy also. It may also have lost impact with the boy smaller in the phot also I am not sure. Just a suggestion. Thanks again for your comment. Joe', '2006-07-31 21:32:17.000000', NULL, 0),
(2681729, 365364, 22301, '<td valign="top">Kim, <br/>Thanks for your comment on my on the beach entry and I thought I would return the comment.<br/>Tsweet moment you have captured here. I think the color is good and lighting as well. One thing that stands out right away is the tilted horizon, I think this line should really be horizontal and detratcs from the photo abit. Another thing would have been to shoot from a much lower angle so that the photo felt like it was more from the child''s perspective. Black and white may have been a nice touch but I am not sure I would have done. And one other thing that may have helped your score would to be to have the photo larger, at the 640 px limit. I hope you found these suggestions helpful and thankd again fpr your comment.<br/>joe</td>', 'Kim, Thanks for your comment on my on the beach entry and I thought I would return the comment.Tsweet moment you have captured here. I think the color is good and lighting as well. One thing that stands out right away is the tilted horizon, I think this line should really be horizontal and detratcs from the photo abit. Another thing would have been to shoot from a much lower angle so that the photo felt like it was more from the child''s perspective. Black and white may have been a nice touch but I am not sure I would have done. And one other thing that may have helped your score would to be to have the photo larger, at the 640 px limit. I hope you found these suggestions helpful and thankd again fpr your comment.joe', '2006-07-31 21:46:31.000000', NULL, 0),
(2681808, 364824, 22301, '<td valign="top">Jon, thanks for your comment on my Beach entry and thoguth I would give you a comment in return. This is a cool shot and well composed. I enjoy the colors in the photograph as well. The things I could think about for improvement would be to maybe have shot at more of angle so thatyou could see the birds and down the levee into to the water, or maybe just keep your camera on the birds and when they start to fly away strat taking photos of them. Who know if any of those suggestions are good but just a thought. Thanks again. <br/>Joe</td>', 'Jon, thanks for your comment on my Beach entry and thoguth I would give you a comment in return. This is a cool shot and well composed. I enjoy the colors in the photograph as well. The things I could think about for improvement would be to maybe have shot at more of angle so thatyou could see the birds and down the levee into to the water, or maybe just keep your camera on the birds and when they start to fly away strat taking photos of them. Who know if any of those suggestions are good but just a thought. Thanks again. Joe', '2006-07-31 22:07:50.000000', NULL, 0),
(2681852, 365507, 22301, '<td valign="top">Jason, thans for your comment on my photo for on the beach challenge, I thought i would give the comment back, and I agree with your comment that I could have moved over to get that light out of the way, I am not sure how much I noticed when I shot the photo. I think this shot is great, it was one of only three 9''s I gave out in the whole challenge. The way you processed this image is excellent. My one complaint would be this border that you used, I think using no border or something much smaller would have helped your score go up a few tenths higher. Thanks again for your comment and this is an excellent image,<br/>joe</td>', 'Jason, thans for your comment on my photo for on the beach challenge, I thought i would give the comment back, and I agree with your comment that I could have moved over to get that light out of the way, I am not sure how much I noticed when I shot the photo. I think this shot is great, it was one of only three 9''s I gave out in the whole challenge. The way you processed this image is excellent. My one complaint would be this border that you used, I think using no border or something much smaller would have helped your score go up a few tenths higher. Thanks again for your comment and this is an excellent image,joe', '2006-07-31 22:27:40.000000', NULL, 0),
(2681936, 365394, 22301, '<td valign="top">Amy, thanks for my on the beach entry and I thought i would return the comment. I think this is a good beach shot but i think I would have liked to  have seen more beach and not sucha tight crop. Maybe a wider shot with the shell not placed so directly in the middle of the photo. Also I know you didn''t have access to photoshop but the size of your photo file is really small 35 kb, but having your file closer to the 150 kb max would have helped abit.  Just some suggestions, and thanks for your comment, joe.</td>', 'Amy, thanks for my on the beach entry and I thought i would return the comment. I think this is a good beach shot but i think I would have liked to  have seen more beach and not sucha tight crop. Maybe a wider shot with the shell not placed so directly in the middle of the photo. Also I know you didn''t have access to photoshop but the size of your photo file is really small 35 kb, but having your file closer to the 150 kb max would have helped abit.  Just some suggestions, and thanks for your comment, joe.', '2006-07-31 22:47:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(2682208, 365342, 22301, '<td valign="top">Ingrid,<br/>Thanks for the comment on my on the beach entry I thoguth I would give you one also.<br/>This is a cool shot, I really like the ocean spray you captured. My only suggestion would be to maybe have shot at a wider angle and used the rule of thirds to place the trap. I thin you did a really good job of actually finding something at the beach. Thans again for the comment.<br/>Joe</td>', 'Ingrid,Thanks for the comment on my on the beach entry I thoguth I would give you one also.This is a cool shot, I really like the ocean spray you captured. My only suggestion would be to maybe have shot at a wider angle and used the rule of thirds to place the trap. I thin you did a really good job of actually finding something at the beach. Thans again for the comment.Joe', '2006-08-01 00:07:08.000000', NULL, 0),
